@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix pmp:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/practices/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the second protocol opened against a generalization, and the
# second one to come back with a deviation from the holding's own material
#
# The claim examined was written on 2026-09-15 and its falsifying condition was
# stated in the same node: a transmission that prescribes a bodily operation,
# carries rules for the persons performing it, and carries none about what they
# have eaten, drunk or felt. That condition was not met. What was found instead
# is a case the claim did not provide for and which its wording excludes: the
# same variable, named in the opposite direction.
#

pmp:BodilyConditionDirectionExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the intake condition is always stated as a prohibition"@en, "Ob die Aufnahmebedingung immer als Verbot gesetzt ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "A protocol on the claim that where a transmission prescribes an operation on a body and addresses any rule to the persons taking part, those rules include states of recent intake and of strong feeling under which the operation is not to be performed. The question put to the holding was not whether the variable recurs — the claim was formed from four witnesses that it does — but whether the direction does."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheBodyOfThePartiesIsAStatedCondition ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "A transmission that prescribes a bodily operation, carries rules for the persons performing it — purity, dress, posture, hour — and carries none about what they have eaten, drunk or felt. That is the condition the claim itself named, and it was not met by anything found here. The deviation reported below falsifies a different clause, the one that fixes the direction."@en ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched the holding for every transmission carrying a rule about eating, drinking, continence or strong feeling addressed to a person taking part in an operation, by term over all tradition files and by reading the nodes returned. Twelve files carry such rules. Of those read, four state the condition as a prohibition and are the witnesses the claim was formed from; five state it as a prescription and are the deviation; one corpus states it in both directions within itself. Not searched: the grimoire and ritual literature outside this holding, where the falsifying case the claim names would have to be found, and no modern study was consulted, so no pm:evidenceFrom is set."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, 2026-09-16" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-16" ;
    skos:note "The deviation, stated as the cases. The Solomonic operator does not avoid an empty stomach, he is required to reach one: nine days of continence before the circle, the last three on fasting diet once a day, better bread and water only, and nothing at all on the last, with the general clause of the book quoted as the cost of any defect (pmc:ClaviculaNovemDies). Whoever enters a greater Andean office fasts a month, in other places six, in others a year, without salt and without chilli (pmp:FastBeforeTakingOffice, pmc:OfficePresupposesTheFast). The Maya fast before the first month has a stated floor of thirteen days, with continence and the giving up of salt and chili added, and a stated consequence for breaking one begun (pmp:MayaNewYearFast, pmc:MayaSafetyPresupposesUnbrokenFast). The Jain transmission counts its fasting in meals rather than days and puts the abstention from well-dressed food and from eating to excess among the ten conditions of its celibacy, with a named consequence at each (pmp:JainaMahaviraUpadhana, pmp:JainaDashaBrahmacaryasamadhi, pmc:JainaBrahmacaryabhangaPhala)."@en ;
    skos:note "One corpus carries both directions and it is already one of the four witnesses, which is the sharpest form the deviation could take. The Ars Notoria warns that no man presume to say the oration after too much drinking or luxury, nor fasting, without great reverence and discretion; and the same corpus prescribes fasting for the first day of a rehearsal. The node that carries the warning said so when it was written and left the two passages unreconciled. A claim formed from that witness and reading only its prohibitive half took the witness at half its word."@en ;
    skos:note "What survives. The first two sentences of the claim stand and are strengthened rather than weakened: the variable is recent intake and strong feeling, it attaches to a person present rather than to the day, the place or the material, and it recurs across transmissions with no route between them — now across nine and not four. The clause that fails is the one that fixes the direction, and it fails because it was read off a set of witnesses selected from the caution layer. A claim formed from cautions will find prohibitions, which is a property of where one looked and not of what transmissions do."@en ;
    skos:note "The third sentence of the claim is untouched by this protocol and is not thereby confirmed. It says that where such a transmission states what follows from proceeding anyway, it states a disorder of the body and not a failure of the operation. Two of the deviating cases state a consequence and neither is a bodily disorder: the Solomonic clause makes the experiment not verified, and the Maya one makes evil come upon the faster or his house. That is contrary material and it is recorded here rather than folded into the deviation above, because the two clauses fail for different reasons and a later reader should be able to take them apart."@en ;
    skos:note "What the protocol is worth for examination, which is the reason to run it at all. The reformulated variable is still the one an experiment can set, and the deviating cases make it more so, because several of them carry a countable term: nine days with three inside them, thirteen days at the least, one month or six or a year, the sixth meal or the eighth or the tenth. One of them is even checked after the fact by the transmission itself — the Andean guinea-pig inspection that reads off whether the fast was kept (pmc:FastKeptWellIsAlsoChecked). A transmission that checks its own participants supplies the procedure a protocol would otherwise have to invent."@en ;
    skos:note "The finding about this project's procedure, which is the same one prima-materia#667 recorded and is therefore not new but recurrent. The counter-search for this claim was carried before it was written, as SPEC §16 requires, and it was carried in the wrong layer: it asked which transmissions name no such condition and it did not ask which name it the other way round. A counter-search that looks only for the absence of the antecedent will never find a case that satisfies the antecedent and reverses the consequent. The general form is worth keeping: for a claim of the shape where X, the rule is Y, the negation to search is not only no rule but also the rule not-Y."@en .
